
### Auth Decorators
```python
@auth_required      # Any logged-in user
@admin_required     # User with role="admin"
@role_required("editor")  # Specific role
```

### Signed-in pages in tests
```python
from feather.testing import login_as

def test_dashboard(app, client):
    with app.app_context():
        user = User(email="owner@test.com", username="owner", active=True)
        db.session.add(user)
        db.session.commit()
        login_as(client, user)
    assert client.get("/dashboard").status_code == 200
```
Sessions use "strong" protection, so a session set by hand is signed out on the
next request. `login_as` sets it the way a real sign-in would. Works with
`csrf_client` too.

### How people sign in
`SIGN_IN_METHOD` in config.py is `"google"` or `"email"`. Email sends a
single-use link that expires in 15 minutes (`/auth/email/login`), needs no
Google Cloud project, and follows the same approval rules as Google. The email
goes through `SIGN_IN_RELAY_URL` + `SIGN_IN_RELAY_TOKEN` when set (hosting
platforms such as Appentic set them), else Resend (`RESEND_API_KEY`), else the
link is written to the log. Send people to sign in with
`url_for('email_auth.login')` or `url_for('google_auth.login')` to match.
